For a production microservices system, configuration should not be stored inside each service. Instead, we use a centralized configuration server. In Spring ecosystem this is done using Spring Cloud Config.

You already learned Service Discovery with Netflix Eureka in Day 33. Config Server is the next step to make your architecture production-ready.

Day 35 – Config Server (Centralized Configuration)
1️⃣ Problem Without Config Server

Suppose you have 5 services:

Account Service
Transaction Service
Notification Service
Auth Service
API Gateway

Each service has its own application.yml.

Problems:

Config duplication

Hard to update database URLs

Restart every service for changes

Difficult environment management

Example:

account-service → db.url
transaction-service → db.url
notification-service → db.url

Updating DB credentials means changing multiple services.

2️⃣ Solution – Config Server

Architecture:

                +-------------------+
                |   Config Server   |
                | (Central Config)  |
                +---------+---------+
                          |
                          v
                    Git Repository
                          |
      ------------------------------------------------
      |                    |                         |
      v                    v                         v
Account Service     Transaction Service     Notification Service

Config Server reads configuration from Git repository.

3️⃣ Advantages

✔ Centralized configuration
✔ Environment based configs (dev, qa, prod)
✔ No need to rebuild services
✔ Dynamic refresh
✔ Version control with Git

4️⃣ Step 1 – Create Config Server

Create a Spring Boot project with dependency:

Spring Cloud Config Server

Main class:

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableConfigServer
public class ConfigServerApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(ConfigServerApplication.class, args);
    }
}
5️⃣ application.yml (Config Server)
server:
  port: 8888

spring:
  cloud:
    config:
      server:
        git:
          uri: https://github.com/your-org/config-repo

This server will read configuration files from Git.

6️⃣ Config Repository Structure

Example Git repo:

config-repo
│
├── account-service.yml
├── transaction-service.yml
├── notification-service.yml
└── application.yml

Example:

account-service.yml

server:
  port: 8081

spring:
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/accountdb
    username: root
    password: root
7️⃣ Step 2 – Configure Microservice

Add dependency:

Spring Cloud Config Client

Then create:

bootstrap.yml

spring:
  application:
    name: account-service

  cloud:
    config:
      uri: http://localhost:8888

Now when service starts:

Account Service → Config Server → Git Repo
8️⃣ Config Fetch URL

Example:

http://localhost:8888/account-service/default

Response:

account-service.yml configuration
9️⃣ Dynamic Config Refresh

If config changes in Git, services can refresh using Spring Boot Actuator.

Endpoint:

POST /actuator/refresh

Then service loads new configuration without restart.

